From: YOUR NAME <andrew@orospakr.ca>
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Input: psmouse-smbus - identify the device again on resume

Synaptics touchpads driven over SMBus (RMI4 InterTouch) that have spent
a few hours in S3 come back in a state where the first report of every
touch is stale: the frame at touch-down carries the landing position and
the next frame, 15 ms later, already carries wherever the finger has
moved to in the meantime, several millimetres away.  To the user the
cursor sits still after touch-down and then jumps.  Nothing on the SMBus
side clears it: rewriting the F01/F12 configuration, or unbinding and
re-probing rmi_smbus altogether, leaves it in place.

The device recovers as soon as it is talked to over PS/2 with anything
other than the disable that psmouse_smbus_reconnect() already sends: a
Get ID is enough.  That is also what happens on every attach, which is
why reloading psmouse always cured it.

Measured on a ThinkPad T14 Gen 2a (LEN2073, TM3471-030) with the first
frame of single-finger drags after suspends of 2.3 to 22 hours:

                                     first-frame jump   drags ramping
                                     (median, units)    from rest
  after resume                           54 .. 115           0/49
  + F01/F12 config pass                       60             0/6
  + rmi_smbus re-probe                        58             0/6
  + PS/2 disable                             115             0/7
  + PS/2 Get ID                                0             7/7
  + PS/2 identify/capability queries           0             8/8

Send a Get ID before deactivating the device in the reconnect path.
Any PS/2 command invalidates the device's SMBus register mapping, but
the SMBus client is registered after the PS/2 device and therefore
resumes after it, and rmi_smb_resume() clears the mapping cache then,
exactly as it already has to for the disable.

Signed-off-by: YOUR NAME <andrew@orospakr.ca>
---
 drivers/input/mouse/psmouse-smbus.c | 13 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/input/mouse/psmouse-smbus.c b/drivers/input/mouse/psmouse-smbus.c
--- a/drivers/input/mouse/psmouse-smbus.c
+++ b/drivers/input/mouse/psmouse-smbus.c
@@ -117,6 +117,19 @@
 static int psmouse_smbus_reconnect(struct psmouse *psmouse)
 {
 	struct psmouse_smbus_dev *smbdev = psmouse->private;
+	u8 param[2];
+
+	/*
+	 * Synaptics touchpads that spent a few hours in S3 come back in a
+	 * state where the first report of every touch is stale (the landing
+	 * position, then wherever the finger already is 15 ms later) until
+	 * the PS/2 side has been talked to. Deactivating alone does not do
+	 * it, a Get ID does. Like the deactivate, this invalidates the
+	 * device's SMBus register mapping; the SMBus client resumes after us
+	 * and rebuilds its mapping cache then.
+	 */
+	param[0] = 0xa5;
+	ps2_command(&psmouse->ps2dev, param, PSMOUSE_CMD_GETID);
 
 	if (smbdev->need_deactivate)
 		psmouse_deactivate(psmouse);
